<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Enoch Sontonga</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1873–<a href="/years/1905">1905</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Enoch Mankayi Sontonga</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Enoch Sontonga was a Xhosa composer and teacher, born in Uitenhage in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-cape">Eastern Cape</a> Colony, who wrote the hymn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nkosi-sikelel-iafrika">Nkosi Sikelel' iAfrika</a> (God Bless <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>). Trained at the Lovedale Institution, he taught and led the choir at a Methodist mission school in Nancefield, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a>, and composed the first verse and chorus in 1897 as a school anthem; the poet Samuel Mqhayi later added seven verses. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-national-congress">African National Congress</a> adopted it as its closing anthem in 1925, Tanganyika and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a> took versions of it as national anthems, and since 1994 it has formed the first half of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>'s anthem. Sontonga died in 1905, and his grave was declared a national monument in 1996.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/enoch-sontonga.jpg" alt="Enoch Sontonga" width="123" height="175" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sontonga.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
